A||||4||Cooter||Cam timing mark vs. No. 1 TDC???||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||03-28-2000||12:39 AM||||On most GM v8's, when the cam and crank timing marks are both pointed up, that is Number 1 cyl at top dead center. Does this hold true for the v6??? If not, is it when both marks are pointed together no. 1 TDC?<BR>Help!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||03-28-2000||09:18 PM||||Come on now- No one has replaced the camshaft and needed to align the timing marks? Did you put them facing each other or both straight up?||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||03-28-2000||10:03 PM||||Cooter, I'm like you, I have more experience in the V8 department. The Haynes manual says the marks should be pointing towards each other, or in other words, the crank gear in the 12 o'clock position and the cam gear in the 6 o'clock position. This puts #1 and #4 piston in TDC with #4 being in the firing position. Anybody else? Is this right?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||03-29-2000||04:07 AM||||Ok, I give...  Just rebuilt my 2.8 with a performance cam and the marks go towards each other (cam down, crank up) just like the Haynes manual shows.  Started up first time.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||03-29-2000||04:01 PM||||With the cam gear mark pointing down either #1 or #4 will be TDC compression (not sure which). With it up the other will be TDC compression.<P>It doesn't really matter which way it is until you put the distributor in. Then you want #1 TDC compression (both valves closed as the piston comes up). Either it already will be or you will need to rotate the crank one revolution.<P>Hope this helps.<P><BR>||||reg||1
